In article <1991Mar3.102331.18269@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
> david@doe.utoronto.ca (David Megginson) writes:
>> Here are some suggestions for limiting the power of the ring of
>> conflict:
[excellent suggestions omitted for space]
>All good, but someone proposed one a month ago that was even better:
>Pretend the screen is checkerboard colored.
>On even turns, monsters on the same color square as the player attack
>the player, or try to get closer, while monsters on opposite colored
>squares attack some nearby non-player monster if available, the player
>if no monster is available but the player is, and make a random move
>otherwise.
>On odd turns, swap the effect to the other colored squares.
>This makes the ring of conflict still cause dissention among the monsters,
>but the player no longer gets off scott free.  It is easy to implement,
>and restores a lot of game balance.
>Kent, the man from xanth.

I like this with reservations. A monster on the diagonal from the player
will be on the same colour space every turn and so be 50/50 naughty
and nice. However a monster walking towards the player along a rectangular
direction, and starting out mean, will stay mean and head towards the player
until it hits - because it will move one closer, change square colour, and
stay mean.
Secondly, a smart player will be able to predict who's going to attack
him or her this round and take steps to avoid the most dangerous.
How about: every round randomly determine [50% chance per monster]
which ones will attack the ring wearer and which will be conflicted.
Or, just to make the code more complex, the chance to be conflicted
could be influenced by level, monster and player alignment, and 
special features of the monster - mindless ones might be easy to
influence and intelligent ones, especially the wizard, might have
only a small chance of being forced to obey the ring.

Then if monsters were allowed to wear rings of conflict, the player
would have a chance to make a decision from time to time:

[Player is conflicted]
You feel controlled! -more-
You hit Fluffy! -more-
[more hitting and player gets a chance to decide ]
Your head clears.

Players with high Wisdom and Charisma might be harder to control, and
Barbarians might be really easy.

How about stacking - two rings of conflict?
How about a +2 ring of conflict giving a base 70% chance instead of 50%?

